{
  "result": "The best time to be awake is not talked about enough. It's 6:12 AM in early October. The sun hasn't quite committed yet. The air outside your window is blue, not black, not bright. Just blue. \n\nIn that blue, the world is unclaimed. No emails have been sent. No one is disappointed in you yet. Cars haven't warmed up. The coffee you make sounds louder than it should, like it's performing just for you. \n\nI used to think freedom would feel large \u2014 like standing on a cliff or quitting a job. But mostly it feels like this small. A quiet kitchen. Socks that don't match. A to-do list that still sleeps under a magnet on the fridge. \n\nWe spend so much of our lives trying to be impressive. We curate bookshelves we haven't read, playlists for moods we want to have. But at 6:12 AM, nobody's watching. You don't have to be interesting. You just have to be warm. \n\nMaybe that's why I love mornings that aren't quite mornings yet. They remind me that the day doesn't start when the alarm rings. It starts when you decide to notice it. And sometimes noticing is just holding a hot mug with both hands and listening to the house settle, as if it too is just waking up and choosing, again, to stand. \n\nThat's enough reason to get out of bed, I think, even before the blue fades. Tomorrow it will be there again if you want it.",
